        "en": "Market analysts suggest the domestic steel cycle may bottom in the first half of 2026 before entering a gradual recovery in the second half, with iron ore prices projected to stabilize around USD 90–100 per ton in H2, offering some room for cost-side improvement. However, given the typical two- to three-year lag between construction starts and actual output, a meaningful recovery in steel demand tied to reconstruction activity may not fully materialize until 2027–2028, warranting close attention to the gap between fundamental improvement and share price reflection. For Hyundai Steel, with its high exposure to automotive-grade steel, a key point to monitor is whether growth in vehicle production and demand for high-strength EV steel translates into structural benefits. At Korea Zinc, the pursuit of an integrated U.S. smelter project and the contest for control over roughly 10% of shares held by the U.S. government have emerged as new variables in the governance dispute, making the outcomes of upcoming shareholder meetings and board composition critical determinants of both governance and strategic direction. Whether China's steel demand contraction continues to moderate, along with shifts in the U.S. trade environment, will also shape the sector's broader trajectory."
